      "page_idx": 21,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.451,
            0.069,
            0.817,
            0.087
          ],
          "content": "I’m Up and Down, and Round and Round"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.844,
            0.068,
            0.884,
            0.085
          ],
          "content": "113"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.107,
            0.886,
            0.178
          ],
          "content": "Similarly, $\\angle  BCD$ is half the angle subtended by the arc BAD at O. This is the angle denoted by the double arc BOD. Since C is on arc BCD, we need to move from OB to OD via A. So, $\\angle  BCD = \\frac{1}{2} ( \\angle  BOD  )$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.181,
            0.734,
            0.212
          ],
          "content": "$\\mathrm{S}0,\\angle \\mathrm{BAD}+\\angle \\mathrm{BCD}=\\frac{1}{2}$ (complete angle at the centre O)"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.212,
            0.503,
            0.232
          ],
          "content": "A complete rotation at O is $3 6 0 ^ { \\circ } ,$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.239,
            0.588,
            0.271
          ],
          "content": "Hence, $\\angle BAD + \\angle BCD = \\frac{1}{2} \\times (360^{\\circ}) = 180^{\\circ}.$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.273,
            0.743,
            0.294
          ],
          "content": "So, the opposite angles of a cyclic 4-gon add up to $1 8 0 ^ { \\circ } ,$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.301,
            0.59,
            0.322
          ],
          "content": "The converse of this theorem also holds."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.336,
            0.885,
            0.396
          ],
          "content": "**Exercise:** A cyclic quadrilateral has angles measuring $\\angle A = 80^{\\circ}$ $\\angle \\mathrm{B} = 110^{\\circ}, \\angle \\mathrm{C} = 100^{\\circ}$ , and $\\angle \\mathrm{D} = 70^{\\circ}$ . Can such a quadrilateral be drawn? Explain why or why not."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.408,
            0.886,
            0.468
          ],
          "content": "**Theorem 12:** If two opposite angles of a quadrilateral add up to $I 8 0 ^ { \\circ } ,$ then the vertices of the quadrilateral lie on a circle, i.e., they are concyclic."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.478,
            0.445,
            0.709
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.245,
                0.711,
                0.325,
                0.729
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 5.29"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_21_image_10.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.455,
            0.497,
            0.825,
            0.519
          ],
          "content": "**Given:** ABCD is a 4-gon (Fig. 5.29)."
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.456,
            0.522,
            0.689,
            0.543
          ],
          "content": "\\angle BAD + \\angle BCD = 180^{\\circ}.",
          "image_source": "images/page_21_equation_13.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.457,
            0.548,
            0.683,
            0.568
          ],
          "content": "\\angle ABC + \\angle CDA = 180^{\\circ}.",
          "image_source": "images/page_21_equation_14.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.455,
            0.573,
            0.778,
            0.593
          ],
          "content": "**To show that:** ABCD is cyclic."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.454,
            0.598,
            0.877,
            0.715
          ],
          "content": "**Why is this true?** Suppose ABCD is not cyclic. Since A, D, B are not collinear, there is a circle passing through the three points. If that circle does not pass through C, there are two cases: C is outside or C is inside the circle."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.743,
            0.885,
            0.802
          ],
          "content": "We deal only with the first case and leave the other to the reader. Let E be the point where CD meets the circle (see Fig. 5.29). Then ABED is a cyclic 4-gon. So from Theorem 11, $\\angle BAD + \\angle BED = 180^{\\circ}$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.802,
            0.58,
            0.819
          ],
          "content": "We are also given $\\angle BAD + \\angle DCB = 180^{\\circ}$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.82,
            0.884,
            0.86
          ],
          "content": "So $\\angle BCD = \\angle BED$ . But this is not possible as $\\angle  BED$ is the exterior angle of BEC and hence greater than $\\angle  BCE _{ z }$ i.e., greater than $\\angle  BCD$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.859,
            0.551,
            0.879
          ],
          "content": "So, points A, B, C, D all lie on a circle."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
